.TH VNET 4
.SH NAME
vnet \- the network card server (port "nic0")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR nic0 .
.B /bin/vnet
is started on demand by
.BR init (8)
when
.BR netd (4)
looks the port up, with device rights; it stops after netd.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vnet
serves the first network card one of its backends finds:
virtio-net (PCI, legacy or modern, or arm64's MMIO);
on x86-64 Intel's e1000 and e1000e (QEMU's default cards) and Realtek's
RTL8168h;
on arm64 the Raspberry Pi 4's GENET and the Raspberry Pi 5's RP1 Ethernet.
.PP
Receiving: a thread waits for frames and sends them to the network server in
batches (port "net",
.BR NET_FRAMES ):
one message per batch, not per frame.
Sending: frames arrive by IPC straight into the card's DMA memory.
For cards with a cable, a thread checks the link once a second and tells
the network server when it comes up or goes down
.RB ( NET_LINK ),
so it asks DHCP again.
.PP
It is also, for now, the clock-chip reader: x86-64's CMOS clock, arm64's
PL031 (the Raspberry Pis have none).
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B NIC_INFO
rbuf <\- nic_info_t: the MAC address, the MTU, the clock chip's time at boot
(seconds since 1970, and when it was read), the link (1 up, 0 down, \-1:
none to watch). \-> 0.
.TP
.B NIC_SEND
sbuf = frames, each a 16-bit length then the frame. \-> 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR netd (4),
.BR wifid (4),
.BR ifconfig (1)
